  "text": "None of the three answers can be verified from the reasoning given, and I could not check the classification directly.\n\n- **Solution 1 (answer 1)** asserts that exactly one such graph exists but gives no construction or argument for it. Its P7 example, u6-0-1-2-3-4-u5, is not induced, since u6 is adjacent to u5. The claim that μ(C7) contains an induced P7 is true, though. For example, u0-u1-u2-u3-u4-v5-w is induced.\n- **Solution 2 (answer 3)** gives no reasoning at all.\n- **Solution 3 (answer 0)** has a flawed example. In its path u0-w-u1-v2-v3-v4-v5, w is not adjacent to the u-vertices, so that path is not a P7 at all. The conclusion that μ(C7) fails the P7-free condition is still correct, but that alone does not establish that no graph qualifies. The problem's phrasing (\"how many\") also suggests a positive count.\n\nMy recollection is that the published classification of 4-vertex-critical {P7, C3}-free graphs containing an induced C7 gives a finite list of more than one graph, and I think the count is 12. I'm not certain of that figure, though. Whatever the exact count, none of the three proposed values (1, 3, 0) matches what I believe the classification gives, so I judge that no solution is correct.\n\nCHOICE: NONE",
